<h1>Sortation Systems</h1>
<p class="usa-intro">A sortation system automatically routes products to many destinations at high speed using scanning data, routing logic, and mechanical sort mechanisms. Sortation systems combine induct conveyors, barcode/RFID scanners, sort conveyors, and destination lanes, managed by warehouse control software. Published ratings for carrier-based sorters reach about 20,000 items per hour (examples below). The five major sorter types are shoe sorters, pop-up wheel sorters, crossbelt sorters, tilt tray sorters, and bomb bay sorters, each optimized for different product characteristics and throughput requirements.</p>
<h2 id="shoe-heading">Shoe Sorters (Sliding Shoe)</h2>
<p>Shoe sorters (also called sliding shoe sorters) use a conveying surface of slats, each carrying a divert shoe. The shoes normally sit at one edge, leaving a smooth surface. When a divert is commanded, switches beneath the surface steer selected shoes diagonally across the slats (<a href="https://hytrol.com/products/sortation/sliding-shoe-sorter/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Hytrol offers 22° and 30° divert angles</a>), gently pushing the product off the side of the sorter onto the destination lane.</p>
<h3>How Shoe Sorters Work</h3>
<p>Products ride directly on the shoe surface. A scanner upstream reads the product barcode. The warehouse control system (WCS) determines the destination and calculates when the product will reach the correct sort point. At the right moment, the shoes in that zone activate, creating a diagonal divert path. The shoes contact the product and guide it smoothly off the side at the programmed angle. The product slides down a chute or onto a take-away conveyor at the destination lane.</p>
<p>Hytrol rates its sliding shoe sorters at speeds up to 600 FPM, with models from small and midsize packages (ProSort 1100) up to about 350 lb (ProSort 1400LP). Sort rate is set by speed divided by product length plus gap, so short products and tight gaps give the highest rates. Ask suppliers for sort accuracy measured on your product mix, not a generic figure.</p>
<h3>Shoe Sorter Advantages</h3>
<p>Shoe sorters provide gentle divert action with minimal product impact. They handle a wide range of product sizes on a common platform. Destination lanes can be on both sides of the sorter (bi-directional sort). No individual carriers are needed, products ride directly on the surface, maximizing sort density. For flat-bottomed case and carton sorting, shoe sorters are often the most cost-effective high-rate option.</p>
<h3>Shoe Sorter Limitations</h3>
<p>Products must have flat, rigid bottoms to ride on the shoe surface. Polybags, soft packs, and irregularly shaped items may snag on shoe gaps. Very small or very light items may not divert reliably. Each sorter has a minimum product length and weight, so check them against your smallest SKU. For these product types, crossbelt or tilt tray sorters are more reliable.</p>
<h2 id="popup-heading">Pop-Up Wheel Sorters</h2>
<p>Pop-up wheel sorters use banks of small, angled wheels mounted between <a href="/conveyor-types/roller-conveyors/">roller conveyor</a> rollers. When a divert is needed, the wheels pop up above the roller surface and contact the product's bottom. The powered wheels steer the product onto the angled destination spur. When not diverting, the wheels retract below the rollers and the product travels straight on the roller surface.</p>
<h3>Performance</h3>
<p>Each wheel zone serves one destination. Multiple zones along the trunk line each serve a different lane. Throughput depends on product spacing, product length, and wheel actuation time. Belted variants, such as <a href="https://hytrol.com/products/sortation/pivot-wheel/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Hytrol's belted pivot wheel sorter</a>, run products on belts and place pivoting wheels at the infeed and exit of each divert.</p>
<p>Pop-up wheel sorters are the most common sortation technology in mid-size distribution centers and <a href="/industries/packaging-line-conveyors/">packaging facilities</a>. They integrate with standard roller and belt conveyor platforms. See our <a href="/conveyor-types/transfer-conveyors/pivot/">pivot conveyor guide</a> for more on pop-up wheel mechanism details.</p>
<h2 id="crossbelt-heading">Crossbelt Sorters</h2>
<p>Crossbelt sorters use a loop of individual carrier units, each equipped with a short belt conveyor that runs perpendicular (cross) to the main travel direction. Each carrier circulates around the loop at constant speed. Products are inducted onto carriers at induct stations. When a carrier reaches its destination chute, the crossbelt activates and pushes the product off the side into the chute.</p>
<h3>Product Versatility</h3>
<p>Crossbelt sorters handle the widest product range of any sorter type. Small items, polybags, soft packs, tubes, cylinders, irregularly shaped products, and flat items all ride securely on the crossbelt carrier surface. The belt provides positive containment during transport and positive discharge force during sort. Carrier size sets the product limits. <a href="https://www.interroll.com/products/sorters/mx-h-horizontal-crossbelt-sorter" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Interroll's MX-H horizontal crossbelt sorter</a>, for example, handles items up to 50 kg and up to 1,200 x 700 x 700 mm.</p>
<h3>High Throughput</h3>
<p>Interroll rates the MX-H at 4,000 to 20,000 items per hour at speeds up to 2.7 m/s (about 530 FPM). Multiple induct points around the loop maximize carrier utilization. Sort accuracy is high because each product is contained on its own carrier, with no sliding and no interaction with adjacent products.</p>
<h3>Crossbelt Sorter Cost</h3>
<p>Crossbelt sorters represent the highest capital investment among sorter types. Each carrier is an independent unit with its own belt, motor, and controller. Carrier count scales with loop length and throughput. The per-carrier cost plus the loop infrastructure, induct stations, and controls make crossbelt sorters the largest investment of the sorter types. They justify cost in high-volume e-commerce and parcel operations where product variety and sort accuracy requirements are highest.</p>
<h2 id="tilt-heading">Tilt Tray &amp; Bomb Bay Sorters</h2>
<h3>Tilt Tray Sorters</h3>
<p>Tilt tray sorters use a loop of individual tray carriers that tilt to one side (left or right) to dump products into destination chutes. Each tray is a flat platform mounted on a pivot. The tray tilts at the destination point, and gravity slides the product off into the chute below. Tilt trays suit items that stay on the tray during transport without containment. <a href="https://www.beumergroup.com/products-systems/logistics-systems/bg-sorter-et-tilt-tray/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">BEUMER rates its BG Sorter ET tilt-tray</a> for items from 100 g to 50 kg at speeds up to 3 m/s (590 FPM).</p>
<p>Tilt tray sorters cost less per carrier than crossbelt sorters because the tilt mechanism is simpler than a motorized belt. Tilt trays excel in apparel distribution (folded garments), small parts (cosmetics, electronics), and pharmaceutical order fulfillment.</p>
<h3>Bomb Bay Sorters</h3>
<p>Bomb bay sorters (also called trap door sorters) use carriers with a hinged bottom that opens downward to drop products into destination chutes directly below. The carrier travels over the destination position, the doors open, and the product falls into the chute. The doors close and the carrier continues to the induct station for the next product.</p>
<p>Bomb bay and split tray sorters handle small, light items that can tolerate a short free-fall drop: pharmaceutical cartons, cosmetic packages, small electronics, and books. <a href="https://www.interroll.com/products/sorters/mt-s-split-tray-sorter" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Interroll's MT-S split tray sorter</a>, for example, carries items up to 8 kg and up to 600 x 400 x 200 mm at up to 1.5 m/s, with throughput up to 21,600 items per hour. They offer compact destination spacing because chutes are directly below the sorter rather than beside it.</p>
<p>Bomb bay sorters save floor space by stacking destinations vertically (below the sorter loop) rather than spreading them horizontally (alongside the sorter). This makes them the preferred choice in space-constrained facilities with many sort destinations.</p>
