<h2 id="faq-heading">Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
<div class="usa-accordion usa-accordion--bordered usa-accordion--multiselectable" data-allow-multiple="">
<h3 class="usa-accordion__heading"><button type="button" class="usa-accordion__button" aria-expanded="true" aria-controls="faq-1">What is a sortation system?</button></h3>
<div id="faq-1" class="usa-accordion__content usa-prose">
<p>An integrated system that routes products to many destinations at high speed using scanning, routing logic, and mechanical sort mechanisms. Types: shoe sorter, pop-up wheel, crossbelt, tilt tray, bomb bay. See our <a href="/conveyor-types/diverting-merging/">diverting &amp; merging hub</a> for simpler divert methods.</p>

</div>
<h3 class="usa-accordion__heading"><button type="button" class="usa-accordion__button" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="faq-2">Shoe sorter vs crossbelt, which do I need?</button></h3>
<div id="faq-2" class="usa-accordion__content usa-prose">
<p>Shoe sorter for flat-bottom cases and cartons at lower cost per destination. Crossbelt for wide product variety (polybags, small items, irregular shapes) with each item on its own carrier. Crossbelt sorters cost more because every carrier has its own belt, motor, and controller. Choose based on your product mix diversity and accuracy requirements.</p>

</div>
<h3 class="usa-accordion__heading"><button type="button" class="usa-accordion__button" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="faq-3">How many sorts per hour can a sortation system handle?</button></h3>
<div id="faq-3" class="usa-accordion__content usa-prose">
<p>It depends on sorter type, model, and layout. Published examples: Interroll rates its MX-H crossbelt sorter at 4,000 to 20,000 items per hour and its MT-S split tray sorter at up to 21,600 items per hour. Hytrol rates its sliding shoe sorters at up to 600 FPM, so shoe sorter rate depends on product length and gap. Actual throughput depends on product mix, destination count, induct rate, and recirculation rate.</p>

</div>
</div>
